/* The unit test's written question: the last item of every unit test, read by a grown-up. */
export const UNIT_ESSAYS = {
  nouns: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Write a paragraph that teaches a younger student about irregular plural nouns. Include at least three kinds (for example f to -ves, -en, base plurals, mutant plurals, or foreign plurals), with an example of each.", "rubric": ["Explains what makes a plural irregular", "Names at least three kinds with correct examples", "Writes in complete, clear sentences a younger student could follow", "Spells every plural correctly"], "model": "Most nouns add -s to show more than one, but irregular plurals follow other patterns. Some words that end in f change to -ves, like leaf and leaves. A few add -en, like child and children. Some stay the same, like one deer and two deer. Others change the vowels inside, like mouse and mice. Words borrowed from other languages may keep their old plural, like cactus and cacti.", "minWords": 40},
  modifiers: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Describe a place you know well, like your room, a park, or a kitchen, in one paragraph. Use at least two adjectives in a row in the right order, one adverb that tells how, and one comparative or superlative. Then name two of your modifiers and the word each one describes.", "rubric": ["Describes one place in a clear paragraph", "Puts two or more adjectives in the right order, with a comma only where it belongs", "Uses an adverb that tells how and a correct comparative or superlative", "Names two modifiers and the word each one describes"], "model": "My grandma’s kitchen is the coziest room in her house. A round wooden table sits under a big sunny window, and the shelves hold bright blue bowls. When she bakes, the warm smell of cinnamon slowly fills every corner. The oven is older than my mom, but it still works perfectly. In my paragraph, “coziest” describes the noun “room,” and “slowly” describes the verb “fills.”", "minWords": 50},
  pronouns: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Write a paragraph about a time you and a friend or family member made or fixed something together. Use at least one possessive pronoun (like mine or theirs), one reflexive or emphatic pronoun (like myself), and one relative pronoun (who, which, or that). Make sure every pronoun points clearly to one person or thing.", "rubric": ["Tells about one shared project in a clear paragraph", "Uses a possessive pronoun and a reflexive or emphatic pronoun correctly", "Uses a relative pronoun correctly (who for people, which or that for things)", "Uses subject and object pronouns correctly, with no vague pronouns"], "model": "Last summer my cousin Nina and I built a birdhouse for my grandma, who loves cardinals. Nina measured the boards, and I cut them myself with Grandpa’s saw. The roof, which we painted bright red, kept sliding off, so Nina and I glued it down twice. When we finished, Grandma said the birdhouse was the best gift she had ever gotten. Now it hangs in her yard, and every time a bird visits, she calls us to say the house is still ours.", "minWords": 50},
  verbs: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Write a paragraph about something you did last weekend. Use at least one verb in the past progressive, one in the present perfect, and one modal verb. Then list which verbs you used for each.", "rubric": ["Writes a clear paragraph about the weekend", "Uses the past progressive correctly (was/were + -ing)", "Uses the present perfect correctly (has/have + past participle)", "Uses a modal verb correctly and labels all three"], "model": "On Saturday I was helping my dad in the garden when it started to rain. I have planted tomatoes every spring, so I knew what to do. Next time we should check the weather first. (Past progressive: was helping. Present perfect: have planted. Modal: should.)", "minWords": 40},
  history: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Pick an event your town, school, or family remembers. Write a paragraph explaining how a historian could tell the story of that event: the question they would ask, at least two sources they would use, one perspective that might be missing, and why comparing the sources matters.", "rubric": ["Names an event and the question a historian would ask", "Names at least two sources and what each could show", "Identifies a missing or different perspective", "Explains why comparing evidence matters"], "model": "Our school remembers the year the gym flooded. A historian could ask how the school recovered. They could use the principal’s letters to families and photos from the yearbook. The janitors’ point of view might be missing, even though they did much of the cleanup. Comparing these sources would show a fuller story than any one of them.", "minWords": 40},
  history2: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Was farming a good change for early humans? Write a paragraph that makes a claim and supports it with at least two pieces of evidence. Then explain one way someone could disagree.", "rubric": ["States a clear claim", "Gives at least two pieces of accurate evidence", "Explains how the evidence supports the claim", "Describes a counterargument"], "model": "Farming helped humans as a group but was hard on many individuals. Farming produced a food surplus, so populations grew and towns formed. But farmers’ skeletons show more disease and shorter heights than foragers’, because their diets depended on a few crops. Someone could disagree by saying the surplus allowed jobs like pottery and writing, which made life better over time.", "minWords": 40},
  history3: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Compare two early agrarian societies you studied (for example Mesopotamia, Egypt, the Indus Valley, or Shang China). Write a paragraph explaining one way they were alike and one way they were different, using evidence.", "rubric": ["Names two societies", "Explains one similarity with evidence", "Explains one difference with evidence", "Connects the comparison to geography or resources"], "model": "Mesopotamia and Egypt both grew beside rivers that flooded and left rich soil, which let farmers grow surplus grain and support cities. They were different in how they were ruled: Egypt was united under one pharaoh, while Mesopotamia was made up of separate city-states that often fought. Egypt’s long, protected river valley made unity easier.", "minWords": 40},
  history4: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Choose a belief system or an empire you studied. Write a paragraph explaining how it spread or held power, using at least two specific examples.", "rubric": ["Names a belief system or empire", "Explains how it spread or held power", "Gives at least two specific, accurate examples", "Explains how the examples connect to the claim"], "model": "Buddhism spread from India across Asia mostly along trade routes. Merchants and monks carried its teachings on the Silk Roads, and travelers like Xuanzang brought texts back to China. As it spread, it changed to fit local cultures, which is why Buddhist temples in China look different from those in India.", "minWords": 40},
  history5: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "After Rome and the Han fell, did people go through a dark age, or did they restructure and build new connections? Write a paragraph that makes a claim and supports it with at least two examples from this unit.", "rubric": ["Makes a clear claim about collapse and restructuring", "Gives at least two accurate examples (Byzantium, feudal Europe, Tang or Song China, the caliphates, the Silk Roads, Indian Ocean or trans-Saharan trade)", "Explains how each example supports the claim", "Uses words like because, however, or for example to connect ideas"], "model": "Rome and the Han did collapse, but people restructured instead of falling into darkness. In the East, the Byzantine Empire kept Roman law and grew rich from trade. In China, the Tang and Song rebuilt a strong government and invented paper money and printing. Trade on the Silk Roads and across the Indian Ocean connected these regions more than ever before.", "minWords": 50},
  history6: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Between 1200 and 1750, regional webs became one global web. Write a paragraph that makes a claim about how the world became more connected, and support it with at least two examples from this unit.", "rubric": ["Makes a clear claim about how connections changed", "Gives at least two accurate examples (the Mongol Empire, the Black Death, printing and the Reformation, ocean voyages, the Columbian Exchange, the silver trade, the Atlantic slave trade)", "Explains how each example supports the claim", "Explains who gained from the new connections and who suffered"], "model": "Between 1200 and 1750, the world went from separate webs to one connected web. The Mongols linked China and Europe by land, but the same routes carried the Black Death. After 1492, ships tied the Americas to Afro-Eurasia. Maize and potatoes fed growing populations in Asia and Europe, but smallpox killed millions of Indigenous people, and millions of Africans were forced across the Atlantic. The new connections brought wealth to some and terrible losses to others.", "minWords": 50},
  history7: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "What was the most powerful engine of change from 1750 to 1914: new ideas (like natural rights and nationalism), new machines (industrialization), or new empires? Write a paragraph that makes a claim and supports it with at least two examples from this unit.", "rubric": ["Makes a clear claim about one engine of change", "Gives at least two accurate examples from the unit (a revolution, Italy or Germany, factories in Britain, Meiji Japan, reform movements, imperialism or resistance)", "Explains how each example supports the claim", "Connects ideas with words like because, however, or for example"],"model": "New machines were the most powerful engine of change, because industrialization reached almost every part of life. Factories in Britain pulled families into fast-growing cities, and workers formed unions to fight for better conditions. Industry also gave nations like Britain and Japan the steamships and weapons to build empires. However, new ideas mattered too, because colonized people used ideas of rights and nationhood to resist.", "minWords": 50},
  history9: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Choose one global connection since 1900 from this unit, such as a product’s supply chain, the shipping container, the United Nations, the Green Revolution, or the movement of refugees. Write a paragraph that explains how it connected people in different parts of the world. Use at least two facts or examples, and describe one benefit and one challenge that people have pointed out.", "rubric": ["Names one global connection from the unit", "Explains how it connected people in different places", "Uses at least two accurate facts or examples", "Describes one benefit and one challenge that people have pointed out"], "model": "The shipping container, first used in 1956, connected factories and shoppers around the world. Because goods could move from ship to train to truck in one sealed box, loading became much faster and cheaper. This helped companies build supply chains across many countries, like phones designed in California and put together in China. Supporters point out that this made many goods cheaper for families. Critics point out that some factory jobs moved away from towns that had depended on them.", "minWords": 50},
  bio1: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Explain how your body gets energy from a meal. Follow the food from your mouth to your cells, naming at least four organs and what each does, and say where the energy is finally released.", "rubric": ["Names at least four digestive organs in order", "Explains the job of each organ correctly", "Explains that nutrients are absorbed into the blood and carried to cells", "Says mitochondria release energy in the cells"], "model": "Digestion starts in the mouth, where teeth chew food and saliva begins breaking down starch. The esophagus moves food to the stomach, which churns it with acid. In the small intestine, nutrients are absorbed through villi into the blood, and the large intestine absorbs water. The blood carries nutrients to every cell, where mitochondria release the energy in the food.", "minWords": 50},
  bio2: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "You are the new manager of the Pet World Petting Zoo. Write a paragraph explaining how the zoo can raise healthy baby animals and keep its garden and duck pond healthy. Use at least three ideas from this unit: inherited and acquired traits, sexual and asexual reproduction, animal behaviors that help young survive, plant structures and pollinators, or how the environment affects growth.", "rubric": ["Uses traits or reproduction correctly (offspring inherit genes from two parents; clones are identical)", "Explains an animal behavior and how it helps offspring survive", "Explains a plant structure or pollinator and how it helps the plant reproduce", "Explains how an environmental factor (food, light, space, nutrients) changes growth"], "model": "Baby goats inherit traits like coat color from both parents, so each kid is a little different, which helps the herd survive a new disease. Our hens sit on their eggs to keep them warm, so we give them quiet nest boxes. In the garden, bright squash flowers attract bees that carry pollen, so we plant wildflowers to keep bees around. Finally, growth depends on the environment: the lambs need enough food and space, and we keep fertilizer out of the duck pond so algae don’t bloom and use up the oxygen the fish need.", "minWords": 50},
  prepositions: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "A friend is coming over for the first time. Write a paragraph of directions from your front door to a favorite spot in or around your home. Use at least five prepositional phrases and one interjection, punctuated correctly, and keep every subject and verb in agreement.", "rubric": ["Gives clear directions a friend could follow", "Uses at least five correct prepositional phrases", "Uses one interjection with the right punctuation", "Keeps subjects and verbs in agreement, even with a phrase in between"], "model": "Come through the front door and walk past the stairs. Turn left at the kitchen, and go out the back door into the yard. The swing under the big oak tree is my favorite spot. The rope on both sides of the seat is a little scratchy, but don’t worry. Hooray, you made it!", "minWords": 50},
  bio3: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "You are the head tour guide at the Pet World Greenhouse. Write a paragraph that follows energy and matter from the sunlight on a flower to the wings of a hummingbird that drinks its nectar. Use photosynthesis and cellular respiration, and name what goes in and what comes out of each.", "rubric": ["Explains photosynthesis: light energy, carbon dioxide, and water make sugar and oxygen in the chloroplasts", "Explains that the sugar (in the nectar) carries the stored energy to the hummingbird", "Explains cellular respiration: sugar and oxygen give off carbon dioxide and water and release energy in the mitochondria", "Says the atoms are rearranged, not destroyed (or that the carbon dioxide can go back to plants)"], "model": "It all starts with sunlight. In the flower’s chloroplasts, light energy turns carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil into sugar, and oxygen goes out into the greenhouse. The flower stores some of that sugar in its nectar. When the hummingbird drinks the nectar, the sugar travels in its blood to its wing muscles. There, the mitochondria use oxygen to break the sugar down, which releases the energy to flap its wings and gives off carbon dioxide and water. The atoms are only rearranged, so the carbon dioxide the bird breathes out can be used by the flower again.", "minWords": 50},
  punctuation: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "Design a new superhero for the factory. Write a paragraph introducing your hero: name, powers, and a short scene with dialogue. Use at least one question, one exclamation, a list, an introductory phrase, an appositive, a contraction, and a possessive, all punctuated correctly.", "rubric": ["Introduces a hero with a name and powers in a clear paragraph", "Uses end marks correctly, including a question and an exclamation", "Uses commas correctly in a list, after an introduction, around an appositive, and in dialogue", "Uses apostrophes correctly in a contraction and a possessive"], "model": "Meet Ripple, a hero who controls water. Ripple’s powers include making waves, freezing puddles, and calling the rain. One morning, a fire broke out at the factory. “Can you stop it?” asked the mayor. “I’m on my way!” Ripple shouted. In seconds, the flames were out, and the robots cheered.", "minWords": 60},
  colons: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "The Pause Library is opening a new reading room. Write a paragraph inviting kids to visit. Use at least one colon, one semicolon, the title of a book or movie (underline it if you write by hand) and the title of a song, poem, or chapter in quotation marks, a set of parentheses, a dash, and a hyphenated describing word, all punctuated correctly.", "rubric": ["Writes a clear, inviting paragraph about the reading room", "Uses a colon after a complete sentence and a semicolon between two complete sentences", "Formats a whole-work title and a short-work title correctly", "Uses parentheses, a dash, and a hyphenated describing word correctly"], "model": "Come visit the new reading room at the Pause Library: it has beanbags, lamps, and a window seat. The shelves are full; the beanbags are empty. You can read Charlotte’s Web in the corner (the comfy corner by the window) or listen to “Rainy Day Song” on headphones. On Fridays, the librarian reads a chapter out loud—with silly voices! It’s a well-lit, quiet place to get lost in a story.", "minWords": 60},
  sentences: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "The Sentence Library is holding a writing contest about a favorite place. Write a paragraph about a place you love. Include at least one question, one command, a compound sentence, a complex sentence, a compound-complex sentence, a sentence with an indirect object, and a relative clause.", "rubric": ["Writes a clear paragraph about one place", "Uses a question and a command with the right end marks", "Uses a compound, a complex and a compound-complex sentence correctly", "Uses an indirect object and a relative clause correctly"], "model": "Have you ever been to my grandma’s porch? It looks out over a creek that runs all summer. Grandma reads in the rocking chair, and I sit on the steps. When the sun goes down, the frogs start singing. When it gets dark, Grandma lights a lantern, and we tell stories. Sometimes she hands me a glass of lemonade. Come visit, and you’ll see why I love it.", "minWords": 60},
  conventions: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "The Rule Library is looking for a new helper. Write a paragraph telling a new helper how to spend their first day at the library. Use at least one sentence that starts with a describing phrase like “Walking in the door,” one list of three things in the same form, and one sentence where a phrase like “of the books” sits between the subject and the verb. Every sentence must be complete, with no run-ons.", "rubric": ["Writes a clear paragraph of advice for a new helper", "Uses complete sentences with no fragments, run-ons, or comma splices", "Keeps every verb and pronoun in agreement, including one with a phrase between the subject and the verb", "Uses an opening describing phrase that describes the right doer, and a parallel list of three"], "model": "Walking in the door on your first day, you will see the big front desk. The stack of returned books on the desk needs to be sorted first. Each of the carts has its own label, so match every book to its cart. After lunch, you will dust the shelves, check the computers, and help kids find stories. When the library closes, the chief librarian and the other helpers always thank their new helper.", "minWords": 60},
  history8: {"type": "essay", "prompt": "The historian Erik Hobsbawm called the years from 1914 to 1991 an “Age of Extremes.” Do you agree? Write a paragraph that makes a claim and supports it with at least two examples from this unit.", "rubric": ["Makes a clear claim about whether 1914 to 1991 was an age of extremes", "Gives at least two accurate examples (the world wars, total war, fascism, the Holocaust, the atomic bomb, the Cold War, decolonization)", "Explains how each example supports the claim", "Uses words like because, however, or for example to connect ideas"], "model": "I agree that 1914 to 1991 was an age of extremes. Two world wars were total wars that put whole countries to work and killed tens of millions of people, most of them civilians in World War II. Extreme ideas like fascism led to dictatorships and to the Holocaust, the Nazi murder of six million Jewish people. However, the period also brought new hope, because dozens of colonies won their independence and the United Nations was created to keep the peace.", "minWords": 50},
};
